  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-THA826</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Thailand has ratified 21 ILO Conventions and 2 Protocols, with the majority in force, including 7 fundamental Conventions and one governance Convention. However, progress on ratifying key fundamental Conventions, such as those on freedom of association (C87) and collective bargaining (C98), has been slow. Thailand has recently ratified the Occupational Safety and Health Convention (C155) and its 2002 Protocol, strengthening its commitment to workplace safety and health.

As part of the Thailand Decent Work Country Programme 2023-27, the government and social partners aim to enhance Thailand&apos;s capacity to comply with international labour standards, paving the way for full participation in the global economy. The plan focuses on advancing ratification of C87 on freedom of association and C98 on collective bargaining, improving social dialogue, and addressing child labour and forced labour through national actions and advocacy. Additionally, the government seeks to further strengthen occupational safety and health policies to ensure safer workplaces, addressing both physical and psychosocial risks.</narrative>
  </description>
